%S A401249 1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,1,1,3,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,4,1,1,1,1,
%T A401249 1,3,1,1,1,2,1,4,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,1,1,4,1,2,1,1,1,4,1,1,1,1,1,4,1,1,
%U A401249 1,1,1,3,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,2,1,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,6,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,3,1,2,1,2,1
%N A401249 Minimum number of odd terms in a 2-dense sublist of divisors of n.
%C A401249 a(n) is also the smallest number in the n-th row of A384225.
%C A401249 Conjecture: a(n) is the minimum number of subparts in a part of the symmetric representation of sigma(n).
%C A401249 For more information about the conjecture see A279387.
%C A401249 Observation: at least for the first 200 terms this sequence coincides with the column 1 and also with the right border of A384225. - _Omar E. Pol_, Oct 28 2026
%H A401249 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A401249/b401249.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A401249 a(n) = A401248(n) / A001511(n).
%F A401249 From _Omar E. Pol_, Oct 28 2026: (Start)
%F A401249 Conjecture 1: a(n) = A384225(n,1).
%F A401249 Conjecture 2: a(n) = A384225(n, A237271(n)). (End)
%e A401249 For n = 70 the divisors of 70 are 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, 70. There are three 2-dense sublists of divisors, they are [1, 2], [5, 7, 10, 14], [35, 70]. The first sublist has only one odd term. The second sublist has two odd terms. The third sublist has only one odd term. Therefore the minimum number of odd terms in a 2-dense sublist is 1, so a(70) = 1.
%t A401249 A401249[n_] := Min[Map[Count[#, _?OddQ] &, Split[Divisors[n], #2 <= 2*# &]]];
%t A401249 Array[A401249, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 28 2026 *)
%Y A401249 Cf. A000012 is a bisection.
%Y A401249 Cf. A001511, A174973 (2-dense numbers), A237270, A237271, A237593, A279387 (subparts), A384222, A384225, A400194, A400195, A401248, A401250.
%K A401249 nonn,changed
%O A401249 1,6
%A A401249 _Omar E. Pol_, Oct 18 2026
